**Design notes**

| Concern | How it is met |
|---|---|
| **Confidentiality at rest** | AES-256-GCM encryption. A reader of the storage sees `iv ‖ ciphertext + tag`, which reveals only the approximate plaintext length (12-byte IV + 16-byte tag overhead). |
| **Owner / name isolation** | Each secret lives under a unique storage key `ss\u0000secret\u0000<b64 owner>\u0000<b64 name>`. The NUL byte never appears in base-64, so the key is collision-free even when owners or names contain `:`, `/`, spaces, newlines, etc. |
| **Tamper detection** | The storage key is passed as **Additional Authenticated Data (AAD)** to GCM. Any byte-flip, truncation, or copy of another secret's blob causes the GCM tag check to fail, so `get` throws an `Error`. |
| **Wrong master password** | A different password derives a different AES key; every GCM decryption will fail its tag check and `get` throws. (`createSecretStore` itself does not reject, which the spec permits.) |
| **All state in storage** | No in-memory or file-based state; a new `createSecretStore` call over the same storage with the same password can read every previously stored secret. `remove` calls `storage.delete`, so the data is gone. |
| **No imports** | Only Web Crypto globals (`crypto.subtle`, `crypto.getRandomValues`, `btoa`, `atob`, `TextEncoder/TextDecoder`) are used. |

